If I need a doctor, and every one of the zillion doctors practicing
today on this planet were to die, or decided to retire from practice,
then the meaning of "I need a doctor" would not change. It is
a statement that uses only the intension of "doctor" and not the
circumstantial instantiations on this planet today.

As I said, the "quantification ranges over this planet" principle is a 
Gricean default, not a requirement. You can wish for a gold toilet -- 
it's just pointless. If someone kills all the doctors, you can still 
want or seek a doctor, but it's pointless to say it. Needing is not so 
pointless, I admit. But if you know the quantification isn't going to 
be met on this planet, then we might as well go straight to mi nitcu 
leka ce'u mikce



